  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 310 AM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nOverall quiet and seasonably warm conditions will continue through \nthe weekend and into next week, albeit with some temperature \nregression at times as we work through the new work week. \nPrecipitation chances early-mid week continue to trend down/dry, \npushing best widespread precipitation chances into next weekend when \nthe large scale pattern looks to become more active and favorable \nthrough the region. \n\nCurrently, not much notable change in conditions vs 24 hours ago \nover the immediate area. The mid-level/500mb ridge has shifted \nsubtly eastward, its axis now centered over the area and up into the \nwestern Great Lakes, resulting in a general uptick in, but still \nlargely scattered, mid-upper level cloud cover. Through the weekend, \ncentral CONUS ridge will begin to de-amplify in response to northern \nstream shortwave activity and continue to gradually shift eastward \nwith the approach of cut-off low currently over the California-\nMexico border (seen nicely on WV imagery). Deterministic and \nensemble guidance remain in strong agreement in the overall \nprogression of these features, which will eventually result in a \nsurface boundary draped over portions of the central to northern \nPlains, but north of the CWA. Overall this leaves the immediate area \nremaining under the influence of the mid-level ridge and the western \nreaches of the large eastern CONUS surface high. All to say, expect \nmore of the same quiet, dry, and seasonally warm conditions. Highs \npredominantly in the upper 70s to low 80s and southerly prevailing \nwinds. \n\nAforementioned mid-level cut-off low will move through the \nIntermountain West and become an open wave by Monday, and continue \nto break down the mid-level ridge. Model trends continue to push the \nbest lift and surface features W and NW of the area, resulting in \nthe continued drying trend for PoPs originally over NE Kansas and NW \nMissouri for Monday afternoon and evening. This also will continue \nthe seasonally warm conditions, albeit likely a couple degrees \ncooler with an uptick in cloud cover for at least western portions \nof the CWA. By Tuesday, mid-level pattern becomes more southwesterly \nover the region but tends to lack any significant shortwaves or low-\nlevel moisture return until latter portions of the week. A depicted \nshortwave Tuesday is most poised to result in increasing cloud cover \nand highs falling into the lower 70s before rebounding some \nWednesday and Thursday. By this point, ensemble guidance begins to \ndiverge more notably, attempting to resolve surface boundary \nlocation and any subsequent precip potential. This is readily noted \nvia NBM 25th/75th MaxT spreads jumping from only 3-4 deg F on \nWednesday to 9-12 deg F Thursday. Meteograms suggest that even if \nthe boundary finds itself over the area, precipitation amounts would \nbe light. The best chances for appreciable and widespread \nprecipitation continues to get pushed back toward next weekend.",
